@startuml fig_cache_stale_fill
!include _style.puml
' Book figure 3 — the stale-fill race and the fence.
' Form: sequence. Evidence status: observed result — it illustrates registered claim
' v2-15 (three shared instances under relaxed freshness) by showing the mechanism its
' wrong reads came from. The figure embeds no rate of its own; the rate is stated in
' the claim card beside it.
title Figure 3 — the stale-fill race and the fence

actor "reader R1" as R
actor "reader R2\n(another instance)" as R2
participant "writer W" as W
database "source\n(database)" as DB
participant "shared cache" as C

group unsafe: "publish after commit" is not enough
  R -> DB : read (snapshot S0)
  DB --> R : committed S0
  W -> DB : write S1 · commit
  DB -> C : invalidate key
  R -> C : publish S0 (fill)
  R2 -> C : read → hits the stale fill
  note right of C
    The fill carries a committed-but-older value;
    a reader in another instance now hits it.
    This is the race the claim's wrong reads came from.
  end note
end

group fenced: the publication is refused
  R -> DB : version / CAS check before publishing
  DB --> R : version moved — refuse the fill
  R -> C : publish nothing
  R2 -> C : read → miss → authoritative read
end

note over R, C
  Each mechanism on the protocol table closes one race:
  the fill lease closes duplicate concurrent fills, the
  fence closes this stale fill, the version token closes
  the hit that never consults the source.
end note
@enduml
